  "Question": "In \\(\\triangle ABC,\\) we have \\(AC = BC = 7\\) and \\(AB = 2.\\) Suppose that \\(D\\) is a point on line \\(AB\\) such that \\(B\\) lies between \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) and \\(CD = 8.\\) What is \\(BD?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\sqrt{3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(4\\)",
    "D": "\\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\sqrt{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The equal sides \\(AC=BC\\) suggest splitting the isosceles triangle down its middle. Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\). Then \\(AM=BM=1\\), and the isosceles-triangle property gives \\(CM\\perp AB\\). This creates two right triangles whose common leg is \\(CM\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle CMB\\), the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nCM^2=7^2-1^2=48.\n\\]\nBecause \\(A,M,B,D\\) occur in that order on the line, \\(\\triangle CMD\\) is also right at \\(M\\). Hence\n\\[\nMD^2=CD^2-CM^2=8^2-48=16.\n\\]\nSince \\(MD\\) is a positive length, \\(MD=4\\). Finally,\n\\[\nBD=MD-MB=4-1=\\boxed{3},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
